From 38c96f5cc821b43b77e62f167fc69d2b71915a0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 19:49:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: enable window-surface vsync to avoid tearing on banked
 VESA hardware

Passage's SDL3 port never calls SDL_SetWindowSurfaceVSync, so it
defaults to SDL_WINDOW_SURFACE_VSYNC_DISABLED. On most platforms this
doesn't matter -- the windowing system's own present mechanism handles
tearing. On this DOS backend's banked-VESA present path, though,
DOSVESA_UpdateWindowFramebuffer only issues a WaitForVBlank() before
the bank-by-bank copy when windata->framebuffer_vsync is nonzero
(SDL/0116's bounded-vblank machinery), and that field is only ever set
via this exact API. Left unset, each frame's flush writes directly
into the buffer the CRTC is actively scanning with no synchronization
at all.

Real banked-VESA hardware (unlike DOSBox-X's software VESA emulation,
which swaps buffers instantaneously and has no scanout-timing model)
can therefore tear wherever the flip happens to land in the refresh
cycle -- and land at a *consistent* row every time, since Passage's
fixed ~15fps main loop phase-locks the flip to roughly the same point
in the cycle frame after frame. This matches a real-hardware video
corruption report at consistent rows that neither a pixel-format fix
nor bank-crossing/blit-conversion analysis explained.

Fix: opt in to normal vsync right after window creation, DOS-only
(other platforms' native present paths don't need this and SDL's own
default is deliberately conservative).
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 10278fe..b92f82f 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -345,6 +345,17 @@ void createScreen() {
         return;
         }
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT: the banked-framebuffer present path writes each VESA
+    // bank window directly into the buffer the CRTC is actively
+    // scanning, with no synchronization unless the window explicitly
+    // opts in to vsync. Without this, real banked-VESA hardware can
+    // tear at whatever screen row the flip happens to be at when it
+    // starts; DOSBox-X's software VESA emulation swaps the buffer
+    // instantaneously and never reproduces it.
+    SDL_SetWindowSurfaceVSync( window, 1 );
+#endif
+
     screen = SDL_GetWindowSurface( window );
 
     if ( screen == NULL ) {
-- 
2.47.3

